course: religion-ethics
lesson: 18
title: The Last Supper and the crucifixion
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In the last week of his life Jesus rode into Jerusalem on a donkey while crowds waved palm branches and shouted «Hosanna» — Christians remember this on Palm Sunday.
explain: He cleared the traders out of the Temple, and at the Passover meal, the Last Supper, he washed his disciples' feet and shared bread and wine as his body and blood.
explain: That night Judas betrayed him for thirty pieces of silver; Jesus prayed in fear in the garden of Gethsemane, «Not my will but yours be done»; and Peter, afraid, denied him three times.
explain: Pontius Pilate found no fault in him but gave in to the crowd, and Jesus was crucified on Good Friday, praying, «Father, forgive them».
explain: Christians believe Jesus died to take away the world's sin and to show God's love, which is why the cross became a sign of hope. For a child it teaches: serve others, stand by your friends, and do what is right even when it is not popular.
fact: On Palm Sunday Jesus rode into Jerusalem on a donkey while crowds waved palm branches and shouted «Hosanna».
fact: Riding a donkey, not a war horse, showed Jesus as a humble king of peace.
fact: Jesus overturned the traders' tables in the Temple, saying, «My house shall be a house of prayer».
fact: At the Last Supper Jesus washed his disciples' feet, which was a servant's job.
fact: The Last Supper was a Passover meal at which Jesus shared bread and wine as his body and blood.
fact: Christians remember the Last Supper in Holy Communion.
fact: Judas, one of the twelve disciples, betrayed Jesus to the authorities for thirty pieces of silver.
fact: In the garden of Gethsemane Jesus prayed, «Not my will but yours be done», while his friends fell asleep.
fact: Peter said three times that he did not know Jesus; then the cock crowed and he wept.
fact: The Roman governor Pontius Pilate found no fault in Jesus but let the crowd have him crucified.
fact: Jesus was crucified on Good Friday and prayed from the cross, «Father, forgive them».
fact: Christians believe Jesus died to take away the world's sin and to show God's love.
fact: The cross was once a cruel Roman punishment and became the main Christian symbol of love and hope.
exercise: How did Jesus enter Jerusalem on Palm Sunday? | Riding on a donkey | Riding on a war horse | Carried in a golden chair | Walking alone at night
exercise: What did the crowds shout on Palm Sunday? | «Hosanna» | «Crucify him» | «Amen» | «Peace, be still»
exercise: Why did Jesus choose a donkey and not a war horse? | To show he was a humble king of peace | Because horses were not allowed in the city | Because the donkey was faster | Because he could not afford a horse
exercise: What did Jesus do to the traders in the Temple? | He overturned their tables | He bought their doves | He asked them to lower their prices | He blessed their money
exercise: What did Jesus say the Temple should be? | A house of prayer | A market for everyone | A palace for the king | A school for scribes
exercise: Whose job was washing feet in Jesus' time? | A servant's | A king's | A priest's | A soldier's
exercise: What does Jesus washing the disciples' feet teach? | True greatness is humble service | Leaders should be served by others | Cleanliness is the most important rule | Disciples must wash before eating
exercise: What did Jesus share at the Last Supper as his body and blood? | Bread and wine | Fish and bread | Milk and honey | Lamb and water
exercise: Which church service remembers the Last Supper? | Holy Communion | Harvest Festival | Baptism | Confirmation
exercise: For how much did Judas betray Jesus? | Thirty pieces of silver | Five loaves and two fish | Twelve gold coins | One silver cup
exercise: What did Jesus pray in Gethsemane? | «Not my will but yours be done» | «Let my people go», as Moses said | «Peace, be still», to the wind and sea | «Feed my sheep», to all the disciples
exercise: What were Jesus' friends doing while he prayed in Gethsemane? | Sleeping | Singing hymns | Fishing on the lake | Running away to Galilee
exercise: How many times did Peter deny knowing Jesus? | Three times | Once | Seven times | Twelve times
exercise: What happened after Peter's third denial? | The cock crowed and he wept | The sun went dark and he hid | Pilate arrested him | Judas laughed at him
exercise: Who was Pontius Pilate? | The Roman governor who let Jesus be crucified | The high priest of the Temple | One of the twelve disciples | The king who tried to kill baby Jesus in Bethlehem
exercise: What did Jesus pray from the cross for the people who crucified him? | «Father, forgive them» | «Father, punish them» | «Father, forget them» | «Father, save me now from the cross»
exercise: On which day do Christians remember the crucifixion? | Good Friday | Palm Sunday | Easter Sunday | Ash Wednesday
exercise: Why do Christians wear and display crosses? | It became a symbol of God's love and hope | It is a lucky charm that keeps away all danger | It shows a person is a priest | It marks the place of a battle
test: My brother says Judas denied Jesus three times and Peter betrayed him for money. What do you put right? | Judas betrayed him for money; Peter denied him three times | Both of them betrayed him for money | Peter betrayed him and Judas wept afterwards | Neither of them did anything wrong
test: «why do Christians call it GOOD Friday if he died, that's well weird». What would a Christian say? | They believe his death showed God's love and takes away sin | It's called that because the weather was good | It's good because it's a day off school | It's good because Judas got the silver
test: A friend from a Hindu family asks why the cross is on your church when it was a punishment. What is a good answer? | Christians believe God turned the worst into a sign of love and hope | Because Christians like punishments | Because the Romans built all the churches | Because it is a sign of the Pope
test: «my mates went quiet when the bigger kids picked on me». Which story fits what they did? | Peter denying Jesus out of fear | The crowds shouting «Hosanna» at Jesus | Jesus overturning the tables in the Temple | Jesus washing the feet of his friends
test: Your form teacher says the class captain should be served by everyone else. Which story challenges that? | Jesus washing his disciples' feet | Judas taking the silver | Pilate washing his hands in front of the crowd | The traders in the Temple
test: «the whole class laughed at him so I did too, everyone was». Whose failure in the story is most like this? | Pilate, who went with the crowd instead of what was right | Jesus, who prayed in the garden | The women who stood near the cross | The donkey that carried Jesus
test: Your sister says Jesus was never scared because he was the Son of God. What does the Gethsemane story show? | He prayed in real fear yet chose to obey | He slept peacefully in the garden until the soldiers came | He laughed at the soldiers | He ran away from the garden
test: A classmate with no religion asks, «Was Jesus ever angry?» Which story shows it? | He overturned the traders' tables in the Temple | He fed five thousand people | He washed the feet of all his disciples one by one | He prayed in Gethsemane
test: «He nicked my rubber and I'll never forgive him, ever.» Which words from the cross would a Sunday-school teacher mention? | «Father, forgive them» | «Hosanna» | «My house shall be a house of prayer» | «Peace, be still»
test: Which order of events is right? | Palm Sunday, the Last Supper, Gethsemane, the crucifixion | The Last Supper, Palm Sunday, the crucifixion, Gethsemane | Gethsemane, Palm Sunday, the crucifixion, the Last Supper | The crucifixion, the Last Supper, Palm Sunday, Gethsemane
test: A judge asks, «What should Pilate have done?» Which answer is best reasoned? | Released Jesus, since he found no fault, even if the crowd was angry | Done what the crowd wanted, since crowds are always right | Nothing, because it was not his business at all | Punished the crowd for shouting at him
exam: Identify the occasion upon which Jesus entered Jerusalem mounted upon a donkey, attended by multitudes bearing palm branches. | Palm Sunday | Good Friday | Pentecost | Ash Wednesday
exam: Ascertain the sum for which Judas Iscariot is recorded as having betrayed Jesus to the authorities. | Thirty pieces of silver | Twelve pieces of gold from the Temple | Five pieces of silver | Forty pieces of bronze
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of Pontius Pilate? | He found no fault in Jesus yet acceded to the demands of the crowd | He found Jesus guilty upon substantial evidence | He released Jesus contrary to the wishes of the crowd | He took no part whatsoever in the proceedings
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established significance of the cross in Christian belief. | A former instrument of punishment, now a symbol of love and hope | A token conferring good fortune upon its wearer | An emblem reserved exclusively for the clergy | A memorial to a military victory
